SE radio is brought to you by the ITE computer society and by it e software magazine online at computer.org/software.

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,109
[Music]

5
00:00:19,119 --> 00:00:26,390
Hey, this is Jeremy Jung for Software Engineering Radio and today I'm talking to Elizabeth Fagiraa.

6
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:32,150
She's a wine developer at Codew Weavers and today we're going to talk about what that is and all the work that goes into it.

7
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:34,470
Elizabeth, welcome to Software Engineering Radio.

8
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,631
Thank you Jeremy. I'm glad to be here.

9
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:44,549
I think the first thing we should talk about is maybe saying what wine is because I think a lot of people aren't familiar with the project.

10
00:00:44,559 --> 00:00:47,510
So Wine is a translation layer.

11
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:49,590
In fact, I would say Wine is a Windows emulator.

12
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,750
That is what the name originally stood for.

13
00:00:53,750 --> 00:01:07,510
It reimplements the entire Windows or you say wind32 API so that programs that make calls into the API will then transfer that code to wine and we allow that Windows programs to run on things that are not Windows.

14
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:13,350
So Linux, Mac OS, other operating systems such as Solaris and BSD.

15
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:23,990
It works not by emulating the CPU, but by re-implementing every API basically from scratch and translating them to their equivalent or writing new code in case there is no equivalent.

16
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,109
I believe what you're doing is you're emulating system calls.

17
00:01:29,109 --> 00:01:34,230
Could you explain what those are and how that relates to the project?

18
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:42,789
So system call in general can be used to refer to a call into the operating system to execute some functionality that's built into the operating system.

19
00:01:42,789 --> 00:01:46,550
Often it's used in the context of talking to the kernel.

20
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:54,630
Windows applications actually tend to talk at a much higher level because there's so much highle functionality built into Windows as opposed to other operating systems that

21
00:01:54,640 --> 00:02:01,990
basically we end up implementing much higher level behavior than you would on Linux.

22
00:02:01,990 --> 00:02:08,150
And can you give some examples of what some of those system calls would be and I suppose how they may be higher level than some of the Linux ones?

23
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:10,150
Sure.

24
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:18,630
So of course you have low-level calls like interacting with a file system, create a file and read and write and such.

25
00:02:18,630 --> 00:02:20,470
You also have highle APIs to interact with a sound driver.

26
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:29,830
There's one I was working on earlier today called X Audio where you actually build this bank of sounds that's meant to be played in a game

27
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:39,190
and then you can position them in various 3D space and the operating system in a sense will take care of all of the math that goes into making that work.

28
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:46,949
That's all running on your computer and then it'll send that audio data to the sound card once it's transformed it so it sounds like it's coming from a certain space.

29
00:02:46,959 --> 00:02:55,509
lot of other things like parsing XML is another big one but there's a lot of things the space is honestly huge

30
00:02:55,519 --> 00:03:07,030
yeah I can sort of see how those might be things you might not expect to be done by the operating system like you gave the example of 3D audio and XML parsing

31
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:18,149
and I think XML parsing in particular you would have thought that that would be something that would be handled by the standard library of whatever language the person was writing their application is so that's interesting that is built into the OS.

32
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:21,190
Yeah. Well, in languages like C, it isn't even part of the standard library.

33
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,790
It's higher level than that.

34
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:29,350
It's you have specific libraries that are widespread but not codified in a standard.

35
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:30,869
But in Windows, they are part of the operating system.

36
00:03:30,869 --> 00:03:36,070
And in fact, there's several different XML parsers in the operating system.

37
00:03:36,070 --> 00:03:40,390
Microsoft likes to deprecate old APIs and make new ones that do the same thing very often.

38
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:47,270
And something I've heard about Windows is that they're typically very reluctant to break backwards compatibility.

39
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,030
So you say they're deprecated, but do they typically keep all of them still in there?

40
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:59,351
It all still works. And that's all things that Wine has to implement as well to make sure that the software works as well. Yeah.

41
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,110
Yeah.

42
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:12,470
And we also need to implement those things to make old programs work because there is a lot of demand at least from people using wine for getting some really old programs working from the early '9s even

43
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:18,629
and that's probably a good thing to talk about in terms of what are the types of software that people are trying to run

44
00:04:18,639 --> 00:04:31,350
with wine and what operating system are they typically using in terms of software literally all kinds any software you can imagine that runs on Windows people will try to run it on wine.

45
00:04:31,350 --> 00:04:35,270
So we're talking games, office software, productivity software, accounting.

46
00:04:35,270 --> 00:04:42,790
People will run build systems on wine just run builder programs using on visual studio running on wine.

47
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:49,510
People will run wine on servers for example like software as a service kind of things where you don't even know that it's running on wine.

48
00:04:49,510 --> 00:05:00,629
really super domain specific stuff like I've run astronomy software and wine design computer system design even hardware drivers can sometimes work on wine there's a bit of a gray area

49
00:05:00,639 --> 00:05:08,790
I think from maybe the general public or at least from what I've seen I think a lot of people's exposure

50
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:20,790
to it is for playing games is there something different about games versus all those other types of productivity software and office software that makes supporting those different.

51
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,909
There's some things about it that are different.

52
00:05:23,909 --> 00:05:36,070
Games, of course, have gotten a lot of publicity lately because there's been a huge push largely from Valve, but also some other companies to get a lot a huge wide range of games working well under wine.

53
00:05:36,070 --> 00:05:37,590
And that's really panned out in a way.

54
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:39,430
I think we've largely succeeded.

55
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:44,070
We've made huge strides in the past several years, 5 10 years, I think.

56
00:05:44,070 --> 00:05:47,749
So when you talk about what makes games different,

57
00:05:47,749 --> 00:05:57,990
I think one thing games tend to do is they have a very limited set of things they're working with and they often want to make things run fast.

58
00:05:57,990 --> 00:05:59,909
So they're working very close to the metal.

59
00:05:59,909 --> 00:06:02,390
They're not going to use an XML parser, for example.

60
00:06:02,390 --> 00:06:05,670
They're just going to talk as directly to the graphics driver as they can, right?

61
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:09,510
And probably going to do all their own sound design.

62
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:15,590
I did talk about that X audio library, but a lot of games will just talk as directly to the sound driver as Windows lets them.

63
00:06:15,590 --> 00:06:20,870
So, this is often a blessing honestly because it means there's less we have to implement to make them work.

64
00:06:20,870 --> 00:06:29,830
When you look at a lot of productivity applications and especially the other thing that makes some productivity applications harder is Microsoft makes them

65
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:39,510
and they like to make a library for use in this one program like Microsoft Office and then say, well, other programs might use this as well.

66
00:06:39,510 --> 00:06:45,830
let's put it in the operating system and expose it and write an API for it and everything and maybe some other programs use it.

67
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:52,710
Mostly it's just Office, but it means that Office relies on a lot of things from the operating system that we all have to reimplement.

68
00:06:52,720 --> 00:07:01,909
Yeah, that's somewhat counterintuitive because when you think of games, you think of these really high performance things that seem really complicated,

69
00:07:01,909 --> 00:07:08,790
but it sounds like from what you're saying because they use the lower level primitives, they're actually easier in some ways to support.

70
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:16,150
Yeah, certainly in some ways they'll do things like reimplement the heap allocator because the built-in heap allocator isn't fast enough for them.

71
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:19,029
It's another good example.

72
00:07:19,029 --> 00:07:22,150
You mentioned Microsoft's more modern office suites.

73
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:30,629
I've noticed there are certain applications that aren't supported, like for example, I think the modern Adobe Creative Suite.

74
00:07:30,629 --> 00:07:32,629
What's the difference with software like that?

75
00:07:32,629 --> 00:07:37,189
And does that also apply to the modern office suite or is that actually supported?

76
00:07:37,189 --> 00:07:41,749
Well, in one case you have things like Microsoft using their own APIs that I mentioned.

77
00:07:41,759 --> 00:07:55,270
With Adobe that applies less, I suppose, but I think to some degree the answer is that some applications are just hard and there's no way around it and we can only spend so much time on a hard application.

78
00:07:55,270 --> 00:07:57,510
Debugging things can get very hard with wine.

79
00:07:57,510 --> 00:07:59,670
Let me explain that for a minute.

80
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:07,029
Because normally when you think about debugging an application, you say, "Oh, I'm going to open up my debugger, pop it in, break at this point,

81
00:08:07,029 --> 00:08:19,589
see what like all the variables are or if they're not what I expect or maybe wait for it to crash and then get a back trace and see where it crashed and why." You can't do that with wine because you don't have the application, you don't have the symbols, you don't have your debugging symbols,

82
00:08:19,589 --> 00:08:28,231
you don't know anything about the code you're running unless you take the time to disassemble and decompile and read through it. And that's difficult.

83
00:08:28,231 --> 00:08:35,190
It's only difficult every time I've looked at a program and been like, I'm going to just try and figure out what the program is doing.

84
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:38,230
It takes so much time and it is never worth it. And sometimes you have to.

85
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:42,310
Sometimes you have no other choice.

86
00:08:42,310 --> 00:08:49,269
But usually you rely on seeing what calls it makes into the operating system and trying to guess which one of those is going wrong.

87
00:08:49,269 --> 00:09:00,710
Now, sometimes you'll get lucky and it'll crash in wine code or sometimes it'll make a call into a function that we don't implement yet and we know, oh, we need to implement that function.

88
00:09:00,710 --> 00:09:08,230
But sometimes it does something more obscure and we have to figure out, well, like all of these millions of calls it made, which one of them are we implementing incorrectly?

89
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:12,949
So, it's returning the wrong result or not doing something that it should.

90
00:09:12,949 --> 00:09:22,470
And and then you add on to that all the sort of harder to debug things like memory errors that we could make and it can be very difficult.

91
00:09:22,470 --> 00:09:27,670
And so sometimes some applications just suffer from those hard bugs.

92
00:09:27,670 --> 00:09:32,631
And sometimes it's also just a matter of not enough demand for something for us to spend a lot of time on it. Right.

93
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:34,949
Yeah.

94
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:39,990
I can see how that would be really challenging because like you were saying, you don't have the symbols, so you don't have the source code.

95
00:09:39,990 --> 00:09:48,870
So, you don't know what any of this software you're supporting, how it was actually written.

96
00:09:48,870 --> 00:09:58,470
And you were saying that a lot of times there may be some behavior that's wrong or a crash, but it's not because Wine crashed or there was an error in Wine.

97
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:04,790
So, you just know the system calls it made, but you don't know which of the system calls didn't behave the way that the application expected.

98
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:11,269
Exactly. I can see how that would be really challenging.

99
00:10:11,269 --> 00:10:13,030
And wine runs so many different applications.

100
00:10:13,030 --> 00:10:21,509
I'm kind of curious, how do you even track what's working and what's not as you change wine?

101
00:10:21,509 --> 00:10:27,030
Because if you support thousands or tens of thousands of applications, how do you know when you've got a regression or not?

102
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:29,750
Great question.

103
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:32,470
Probably over half of wine by like source code volume.

104
00:10:32,470 --> 00:10:39,190
I have to actually check what it is, but I think it's probably over half, is what we call is tests.

105
00:10:39,190 --> 00:10:41,590
And these tests serve two purposes.

106
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:53,030
The one purpose is they are regression tests and the other purpose is they are conformance tests that test that test how an API behaves on Windows and validates that we are behaving the same way.

107
00:10:53,030 --> 00:11:02,630
So we write all these tests, we run them on Windows and write the tests to check what the Windows returns and then we run them on Wine and make sure that that matches.

108
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:14,950
And we have just such a huge body of tests to make sure that we're not breaking anything and that all the code that we get into wine that looks like, wow, it's doing that really? Well, nope.

109
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:16,550
That's what Windows does.

110
00:11:16,550 --> 00:11:19,350
The test says so.

111
00:11:19,350 --> 00:11:23,269
So pretty much any new code that we get, it has to have tests to demonstrate that it's doing the right thing.

112
00:11:23,279 --> 00:11:34,230
And so rather than testing against a specific application, seeing if it works,

113
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:40,470
you're making a call to a Windows system call, seeing how it responds, and then making the same call within Wine and just making sure they match.

114
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:43,351
Yes. Exactly.

115
00:11:43,351 --> 00:11:45,910
And that is a lot more automatable, right?

116
00:11:45,910 --> 00:11:50,470
Because otherwise you have to manually, you know, these are all graphical applications.

117
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:54,310
You'd have to manually do the things and make sure they work.

118
00:11:54,310 --> 00:12:00,471
But if you write automatable tests, you can just run them all and the machine will complain at you if it fails. It continuous integration.

119
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:11,910
And because there's all these potential compatibility issues where maybe a certain call doesn't behave the way an application expects,

120
00:12:11,910 --> 00:12:17,110
what are the types of ways that shows when someone's using software?

121
00:12:17,110 --> 00:12:21,430
I mean, I think you mentioned crashes, but I imagine there could be all sorts of other types of behavior.

122
00:12:21,440 --> 00:12:23,750
Yes, very much so.

123
00:12:23,750 --> 00:12:27,990
Basically, anything you can imagine again is what will happen.

124
00:12:27,990 --> 00:12:33,750
You can have crashes are the easy ones because you know when and where it crash and you can work backwards from there.

125
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:36,389
But you can also it could hang.

126
00:12:36,389 --> 00:12:38,390
It could not render right. Like maybe render a black screen.

127
00:12:38,399 --> 00:12:47,991
For games you could very frequently have graphical glitches where maybe some objects won't render right or the entire screen will be red. Who knows?

128
00:12:47,991 --> 00:12:51,910
In a very bad case you could even bring down your system.

129
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:53,670
And we usually say that's not Wine's fault.

130
00:12:53,670 --> 00:12:57,431
That's the graphics library's fault cuz they're not supposed to do that. no matter what we do.

131
00:12:57,431 --> 00:13:02,150
But sometimes we have to work around that anyway.

132
00:13:02,150 --> 00:13:05,430
But yeah, there's been some very strange and idiosyncratic bugs out there, too.

133
00:13:05,440 --> 00:13:07,190
Yeah.

134
00:13:07,190 --> 00:13:14,950
And like you mentioned that there's so many different things that could have gone wrong that I imagine it's very difficult to find. Yeah.

135
00:13:14,950 --> 00:13:18,310
And when software runs through wine,

136
00:13:18,310 --> 00:13:28,710
I think a lot of our listeners will probably be familiar with running things in a virtual machine and they know that there's a big performance impact from doing that.

137
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:38,790
How does the performance of applications compare to running natively on the original Windows OS versus virtual machines?

138
00:13:38,790 --> 00:13:45,670
So in theory, and I haven't actually done this recently, so I can't speak too much to that, but in theory, the idea is it's a lot faster.

139
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:51,829
So there is a bit of a joke acronym to Wine.

140
00:13:51,829 --> 00:13:57,110
Wine is not an emulator, even though I start out by saying Wine is an emulator and it was originally called a Windows emulator.

141
00:13:57,110 --> 00:14:02,230
But what this basically means is Wine is not a CPU emulator.

142
00:14:02,230 --> 00:14:10,629
When you think about emulators in a general sense, they're often emulators for specific CPUs, often older ones like Commodore emulator or an Omega emulator.

143
00:14:10,629 --> 00:14:20,870
But in this case, you have software that's written for an x86 CPU and it's running on an x86 CPU by giving it the same instructions that it's giving on Windows.

144
00:14:20,870 --> 00:14:26,710
It's just that when it says now call this Windows function, it calls us instead.

145
00:14:26,710 --> 00:14:28,790
So that all should perform exactly the same.

146
00:14:28,790 --> 00:14:42,150
The only performance difference at that point is that all should perform exactly the same as opposed to a virtual machine where you have to interpret the instructions and maybe translate them to a different instruction set.

147
00:14:42,150 --> 00:14:52,629
The only performance difference is going to be in the functions that we are implementing themselves and we try to implements them to perform as well or almost as well as Windows.

148
00:14:52,629 --> 00:15:02,870
there's always going to be a bit of a theoretical gap because we have to translate from say one API to another, but we try to make that as little as possible.

149
00:15:02,870 --> 00:15:14,870
And in some cases, the operating system we're running on is just better than Windows and the libraries we're using are better than Windows, so our games will run faster, for example.

150
00:15:14,870 --> 00:15:19,269
Sometimes we can do a better job than Windows at implementing something that's under our purview.

151
00:15:19,279 --> 00:15:24,470
There are some games that do actually run a little bit faster on Wine than they do on Windows.

152
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:31,350
That reminds me of how there's these gaming handhelds out now.

153
00:15:31,350 --> 00:15:38,790
And some of the same ones, they either let you install Linux or install Windows or they just come with a pre-installed.

154
00:15:38,790 --> 00:15:46,150
And I believe what I've read is that often times running the same game on both operating systems,

155
00:15:46,150 --> 00:15:51,990
running the same game on Linux, the battery life is better and sometimes even the performance is better with these handhelds.

156
00:15:51,990 --> 00:15:56,069
So it's really interesting that that can even be the case.

157
00:15:56,069 --> 00:16:10,629
Yeah, it's really a testament to the huge amount of work that's gone into that both on the wine side and on the side of the graphics team and the kernel team and of course the years of work that's gone into Linux even before these gaming handhelds were even under consideration.

158
00:16:10,639 --> 00:16:18,790
So for people who are familiar with handhelds like the Steam Deck, they may have heard of Proton.

159
00:16:18,790 --> 00:16:24,871
I wonder if you can explain what Proton is and how it relates to wine. Yeah.

160
00:16:24,871 --> 00:16:29,110
So, Proton is sort of a fork, although we try to avoid the term fork.

161
00:16:29,110 --> 00:16:34,389
We say it's a downstream distribution because we contribute back up to wine.

162
00:16:34,389 --> 00:16:37,829
So, it is a alternate distribution fork of wine.

163
00:16:37,829 --> 00:16:46,710
And it's also some code that basically glues wine into an embedding application originally intended for steam and developed for Valve,

164
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:49,670
but it has also been used in other software.

165
00:16:49,670 --> 00:16:58,230
So, where Proton differs from wine besides the glue part is it has some extra hacks in it for bugs that are hard to fix and easy to hack around.

166
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:11,350
It has some quick hacks for making games work now that are like in the process of going upstream to Wine and getting their code quality improved and going through review.

167
00:17:11,350 --> 00:17:13,189
But we want the game to work now when we distribute it.

168
00:17:13,189 --> 00:17:19,990
So that'll go into Proton immediately and then once a patch makes it upstream, we replace it with the version of the patch from upstream.

169
00:17:19,990 --> 00:17:22,870
There's other things to make it interact nicely with Steam and so on.

170
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:25,110
Yeah.

171
00:17:25,110 --> 00:17:30,870
And I think for people who aren't familiar, Steam is like this, I don't even know what you call it, like a gaming store.

172
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:35,270
Call game store game distribution service.

173
00:17:35,270 --> 00:17:37,190
It's got a huge variety of games on it.

174
00:17:37,190 --> 00:17:42,789
And it's a great way for publishers to interact with the wider gaming community just after paying a cut to Valve of their profits.

175
00:17:42,789 --> 00:17:44,390
They can reach a lot of people that way.

176
00:17:44,390 --> 00:17:54,150
And because all these games are on Steam and Valve wants them to work well on their handheld, they contracted us to basically take their entire catalog,

177
00:17:54,160 --> 00:18:01,830
which is huge, enormous, and try and just step by step fix every game and make them all work.

178
00:18:01,830 --> 00:18:06,710
And I guess for people who aren't familiar, Valve Software is the company that runs Steam.

179
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:12,150
And so it sounds like they've asked your company to help improve the compatibility of their catalog.

180
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:14,789
Yes, Valve contracted us.

181
00:18:14,789 --> 00:18:23,350
And again, when you're talking about Wine using lower level libraries, they've also contracted a lot of other people outside of Wine.

182
00:18:23,350 --> 00:18:30,870
Basically the entire stack has had a tremendous tremendous investment by valve software to make gaming on Linux work well.

183
00:18:30,880 --> 00:18:36,230
And when you refer to the entire stack like what are some of those pieces at least at a high level?

184
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:38,549
Let me think.

185
00:18:38,549 --> 00:18:50,950
There is the wine project the Mesa graphics libraries that's another open source software project that has existed for a long time but Valve has put a lot of funding and effort into it.

186
00:18:50,950 --> 00:18:53,430
the Linux kernel in various different ways.

187
00:18:53,430 --> 00:18:57,830
The desktop environment and window manager are also things they've invested in.

188
00:18:57,830 --> 00:19:04,150
Everything that the game needs on any level and that the operating system of the handheld device needs.

189
00:19:04,160 --> 00:19:10,390
