00:23:14,549 --> 00:23:16,230
So there's a couple steps that go into it.

235
00:23:16,230 --> 00:23:29,030
When you think about a program that's a that's a big file that's got all the machine code in it and then it's got stuff at the beginning saying here's how the program works and here's where in the file the processor should start running. That's your exe file.

236
00:23:29,030 --> 00:23:34,390
And then then your DLL files are libraries that contain shared code.

237
00:23:34,390 --> 00:23:43,190
and you have I guess similar sort of file that says here's the entry point that runs this function this parse XML function or whatever have

238
00:23:43,190 --> 00:23:54,950
you and here's this entry point that has a generate XML function and so on and so forth and then the operating system will basically take the exe file and see all the bits in it say

239
00:23:54,960 --> 00:24:07,669
I want to call the parse XML function it'll load that DL and hook it up so the processor ends up just seeing jump directly to this parse xml function and then run that and then return and so on.

240
00:24:07,679 --> 00:24:19,510
And so what wine does is part of wine is a libraries that the implementing that parse xml and readxml function but part of it is the loader which is the part of the operating system

241
00:24:19,510 --> 00:24:24,789
that hooks everything together and when we load we redirect to our libraries.

242
00:24:24,789 --> 00:24:32,149
We don't have windows libraries we we redirect to ours then we run our code and then we jump back to the program.

243
00:24:32,149 --> 00:24:39,190
So it's the loader that's a part of wine that's actually not sure if running the executable is the right term.

244
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:40,789
No, I think that's a good term.

245
00:24:40,789 --> 00:24:49,669
It starts in a loader and then we say okay now run the machine code next executable and then it runs and it jumps between our libraries and back and so on.

246
00:24:49,669 --> 00:24:56,390
And like you were saying before, often times when it's trying to make a system call,

247
00:24:56,400 --> 00:25:07,990
it ends up being handled by a function that you've written in wine and then that in turn will call the Linux system calls or the Mac OS system calls to try and accomplish the same result.

248
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:20,070
Right. Exactly. And something that I think maybe not everyone is familiar with is there's this concept of user space versus kernel space. Could you explain what the difference is?

249
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:27,909
So the way I would describe a kernel is it's the part of the operating system that can do anything, right?

250
00:25:27,909 --> 00:25:32,390
So any code that runs on your computer is talking to the processor.

251
00:25:32,390 --> 00:25:35,750
And the processor has to be able to do anything the computer can do.

252
00:25:35,750 --> 00:25:39,029
It has to be able to talk to the hardware.

253
00:25:39,029 --> 00:25:41,190
It has to set up the memory space.

254
00:25:41,190 --> 00:25:43,029
So actually a very complicated task.

255
00:25:43,029 --> 00:25:47,990
Has to be able to switch to another task and basically talk to another program.

256
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:49,830
And you have to have something there that can do everything.

257
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:53,029
But you don't want any program to be able to do everything.

258
00:25:53,029 --> 00:25:55,350
Not since the '90s.

259
00:25:55,350 --> 00:25:57,269
It's about when we realized that we can't do that.

260
00:25:57,269 --> 00:25:59,590
So the kernel is the part that can do everything.

261
00:25:59,590 --> 00:26:09,669
And when you need to do something that requires those permissions that you can't give everyone, you have to talk to the colonel and ask it, hey, can you do this for me, please?

262
00:26:09,669 --> 00:26:13,669
In a very restricted way where it's only the safe things you can do.

263
00:26:13,669 --> 00:26:15,909
And to a degree, it's also like a library, right?

264
00:26:15,909 --> 00:26:23,669
It's kernels have always existed and since they've always just been the core standard library of the computer that does the things like read

265
00:26:23,679 --> 00:26:36,070
and write files which are very very complicated tasks under the hood but look very simple because all you say is write this file and talk to the hardware and abstract away all the difference between different drivers.

266
00:26:36,070 --> 00:26:40,070
So the kernel is doing all of these things.

267
00:26:40,070 --> 00:26:50,149
So because the kernel is the part that can do everything, when you think about the kernel, it is basically one program that is always running on your computer, but it's only one program.

268
00:26:50,149 --> 00:26:58,310
So when a user calls the kernel, you are switching from one program to another and you're doing a lot of complicated things as part of this.

269
00:26:58,310 --> 00:27:05,990
You're switching to the higher privilege level where you can do anything and you're switching the state from one program to another.

270
00:27:05,990 --> 00:27:13,430
And so this is what we mean when we talk about user space where you're running like a normal program and kernel space where you've suddenly switched into the kernel and

271
00:27:13,440 --> 00:27:22,470
now you're executing with increased privileges and a different idea of the process space and increased responsibility and so on.

