00:27:58,870 --> 00:28:12,870
And so do most applications when you were talking about the system calls for handling 3D audio or parsing XML are those system calls considered part of user space and then those things call the kernel space on your behalf or how would you describe that?

279
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:20,310
So so when you look at Windows most of the Windows library the vast vast majority of it is all user space.

280
00:28:20,310 --> 00:28:23,510
Most of these libraries that we implement never leave user space.

281
00:28:23,510 --> 00:28:25,669
They never need to call into the kernel.

282
00:28:25,669 --> 00:28:29,590
Only the core low-level stuff, things like we need to read a file, that's a kernel call.

283
00:28:29,590 --> 00:28:33,590
When you need to sleep and wait for some seconds, that's a kernel call.

284
00:28:33,590 --> 00:28:38,710
You need to talk to a different process, things that interact with different processes in general.

285
00:28:38,710 --> 00:28:44,950
Not just allocate memory, but allocate a page of memory like a from the memory manager and then that gets suballocated by the heap allocator.

286
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:47,510
So things like that.

287
00:28:47,510 --> 00:28:57,029
So if I was writing an application and I needed to open a file for example, does that mean that I would have to communicate with the kernel to read that file?

288
00:28:57,039 --> 00:29:02,310
Right. Exactly. And so most applications it sounds like it's going to be a mixture.

289
00:29:02,310 --> 00:29:12,789
You're going to have a lot of things that call user space calls and then a few you mentioned more low-level ones that are going to require you to communicate with the kernel.

290
00:29:12,799 --> 00:29:15,110
Yeah, basically.

291
00:29:15,110 --> 00:29:24,710
And it's worth noting that in all operating systems, you're almost always going to be calling a user space library that might just be a thin wrapper over the kernel call.

292
00:29:24,710 --> 00:29:27,750
It might it's going to do like just a little bit of work and then call the kernel.

293
00:29:27,750 --> 00:29:31,830
In fact, in Windows, that's the only way to do it.

294
00:29:31,830 --> 00:29:37,269
In many other operating systems, you can actually say you can actually tell the processor to make the kernel call.

295
00:29:37,269 --> 00:29:44,870
There is a special instruction that does this and it'll go directly to the kernel and there's a defined interface for this.

296
00:29:44,870 --> 00:29:46,789
But in Windows, that interface is not defined.

297
00:29:46,789 --> 00:29:50,710
It's not stable or backwards compatible like the rest of Windows is.

298
00:29:50,710 --> 00:29:53,029
So even if you wanted to use it, you couldn't.

299
00:29:53,029 --> 00:30:01,430
And you basically have to call into the highle libraries or low-level libraries as it were that create a file.

300
00:30:01,430 --> 00:30:03,590
And those don't do a lot.

301
00:30:03,590 --> 00:30:09,029
They just kind of tweak their parameters a little and then pass them right down to the kernel.

302
00:30:09,029 --> 00:30:18,950
And so wine it sounds like it needs to implement both the user space calls of Windows but then also the kernel calls as well.

303
00:30:18,950 --> 00:30:24,549
But wine itself is that only in Linux user space or Mac OS user space.

304
00:30:24,559 --> 00:30:36,230
Yes, this is a very tricky thing but basically all of what is wine runs in the user space and we use kernel calls that are already there to talk to the host kernel.

305
00:30:36,230 --> 00:30:46,630
You get a sort of second nature of thinking about the Windows user space and kernel and then there's a host user space in kernel and wine is running all in the host user space but it's emulating the Windows kernel.

