They don't want anyone hacking them like that and we have to do it anyway for legitimate purposes we would argue to make them work better on more operating systems and so we have to fight that every step of the way.

381
00:38:03,430 --> 00:38:15,750
Yeah, it seems like it's a combination of like you were saying, being able to debug and you're debugging not necessarily your own code, but you're debugging this behavior.

382
00:38:15,760 --> 00:38:26,310
Mhm. And then based on that behavior, you have to figure out, okay, where in all these different systems within wine could this part be not working?

383
00:38:26,310 --> 00:38:36,870
And I I suppose you probably build up some kind of mental map in your head of when you get a a type of bug or a type of crash, you think like, oh, maybe it's this, maybe it's here or something like that.

384
00:38:36,880 --> 00:38:38,950
Yeah, there is a lot of that.

385
00:38:38,950 --> 00:38:45,910
You notice some patterns, you know, experience helps,

386
00:38:45,910 --> 00:38:52,710
but because any bug could be new, sometimes experience doesn't help and you just kind of have to start from scratch.

387
00:38:52,710 --> 00:39:02,790
At sort of a high level, can you give an example of where you got a specific bug report and then where you had to look to eventually find which parts of the system were the issue?

388
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:08,069
One, I think good example that I've done recently.

389
00:39:08,069 --> 00:39:11,349
So, I mentioned this X audio library that does 3D audio.

390
00:39:11,349 --> 00:39:14,470
And say you come across a bug, I'm going to be a little bit generic here.

391
00:39:14,470 --> 00:39:17,910
And say you come across a bug where some audio isn't playing right.

392
00:39:17,910 --> 00:39:19,750
Maybe there's silence where there should be the audio.

393
00:39:19,750 --> 00:39:23,990
So you kind of you look in and see well where is that getting lost.

394
00:39:23,990 --> 00:39:29,349
So you can basically look in the input calls and say here's the buffer it's submitting that's got all the audio data.

395
00:39:29,349 --> 00:39:39,030
You look at where you think the output should be like that library will internally call a different library which programs can interact with directly and this our highle library interacts

396
00:39:39,030 --> 00:39:45,829
with that is the give this sound to the audio driver right so you've got x audio on top of mmdev API which is the other library

397
00:39:45,839 --> 00:40:01,510
that gives audio to the driver and you see the buffers that X audio is passing into MMD dev API they're empty there's nothing in them so you have to kind of work through the X audio library to see where is that sound getting lost. Or maybe that's not getting lost.

398
00:40:01,510 --> 00:40:03,750
Maybe it's coming through all garbled.

399
00:40:03,750 --> 00:40:07,990
And I've had to look at the buffer and see why is it garbled.

400
00:40:07,990 --> 00:40:12,710
I'll open up it up in Audacity and look at the w shape of the wave and say,

401
00:40:12,710 --> 00:40:21,750
"Huh, the shape of the wave looks like we're putting silence every 10 nanose or something or or reversing something or interpreting it wrong, things like that."

402
00:40:21,750 --> 00:40:30,950
You will do a lot of putting in print fs basically all throughout wine to see where does the state change where is it right and then where do things start going wrong.

403
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:34,069
Yeah.

404
00:40:34,069 --> 00:40:45,430
And then the audio example because they're making a call to your X audio implementation you can see that okay the buffer or the audio that's coming in that part is good right?

405
00:40:45,430 --> 00:40:51,990
It's just that later on when it sends it to what's going to actually have it be played by the the hardware, that's when it's missing.

406
00:40:51,990 --> 00:40:55,910
So, we did something wrong in a library that destroyed the buffer.

407
00:40:55,910 --> 00:41:07,030
And I think on a very very high level, a lot of debugging wine is about finding where things are good and finding where things are bad and then narrowing that down until we find the one spot where things go wrong.

408
00:41:07,040 --> 00:41:12,150
There's a lot of processes that go like that.

409
00:41:12,150 --> 00:41:18,151
Like you were saying, the more you see these problems, hopefully the the easier it gets to narrow down where often. Yeah.

410
00:41:18,151 --> 00:41:20,309
Especially if you keep debugging things in the same area.

411
00:41:20,319 --> 00:41:25,990
And wine supports more than one operating system.

412
00:41:25,990 --> 00:41:29,670
I I saw there was Linux, Mac OS, I think FreeBSD.

413
00:41:29,670 --> 00:41:37,670
How much of the code is operating system specific versus how much can just be shared across all of them?

414
00:41:37,670 --> 00:41:40,150
Not that much is operating system specific actually.

415
00:41:40,150 --> 00:41:50,230
So when you think about the volume of wine, the vast majority of it is highle code that doesn't need to interact with the operating system on a low level, right?

416
00:41:50,240 --> 00:41:56,470
Because Microsoft keeps putting lots and lots of different libraries in their operating system.

417
00:41:56,470 --> 00:42:07,430
And a lot of these are highle libraries and even when we do interact with the operating system, we're using crossplatform libraries or we're using pix.

418
00:42:07,430 --> 00:42:14,230
So all these operating systems that we are implementing are basically conformed to the PZIX standard which is basically like Unix.

419
00:42:14,230 --> 00:42:16,390
They're all Unix based.

420
00:42:16,390 --> 00:42:18,630
PZIX is a Unix based standard.

421
00:42:18,630 --> 00:42:25,430
Microsoft is you know the big exception that never did implement that and so we have to translate its APIs to Unix APIs.

422
00:42:25,430 --> 00:42:28,550
Now that said there is a lot of very operating system specific code.

423
00:42:28,560 --> 00:42:36,470
Apple makes things difficult by diverging almost wherever they can and so we have a lot of Apple specific code in there.

424
00:42:36,480 --> 00:42:42,390
Another example I can think of is I believe Mac OS doesn't support Vulcan.

425
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:50,309
Yes, that's a great example of Mac not wanting to use generic libraries that work on every other operating system.

426
00:42:50,319 --> 00:42:58,630
And in some cases we look at it and we're like all right we'll implement a wrapper for that too on top of your operating system.

427
00:42:58,630 --> 00:43:04,309
We've done it for Windows we can do it for Vulcan and then you get the Molten VK project and to be clear we didn't invent Molten VK.

428
00:43:04,309 --> 00:43:06,790
It was around before us but we have contributed a lot to it.

429
00:43:06,800 --> 00:43:20,950
Yeah I think maybe just at a high level it might be good to explain the relationship between Direct 3D or DirectX and Vulcan and yeah maybe if you could go into that.

430
00:43:20,950 --> 00:43:25,990
So, Direct 3D is Microsoft's 3D API.

431
00:43:25,990 --> 00:43:39,990
The the 3D APIs are basically a way to firstly abstract out the differences between different graphics cards, which you know look very different on a hardware level, especially they they used to look very different.

432
00:43:39,990 --> 00:43:43,270
They still do look very different.

433
00:43:43,270 --> 00:43:51,430
And secondly, a way to deal with them at a high level because actually talking to the graphics card on a low level is very very complicated.

434
00:43:51,440 --> 00:43:59,670
Even talking to it on a high level is complicated, but it gets it can get a lot worse if you've ever done any graphics driver development.

435
00:43:59,670 --> 00:44:10,150
So you have a number of different APIs that achieve these two goals of building a common abstraction and of building a a highle abstraction.

436
00:44:10,150 --> 00:44:20,150
So, OpenGL is the broadly the free operating system world, the non-Microsoft's world's choice back in the day and in direct 3D was Microsoft's API.

437
00:44:20,150 --> 00:44:27,510
And both of these have evolved over time and come up with new versions and such.

438
00:44:27,510 --> 00:44:34,230
And when any API exists for too long, it gains a lot of croft and needs to be replaced.

439
00:44:34,230 --> 00:44:41,190
And eventually the people who developed open jail decided we need to start over, get rid of the craft,

440
00:44:41,190 --> 00:44:47,431
make it cleaner and make it lower level because to get into maximum performance games really want low-level access. And so they made Vulcan.

441
00:44:47,440 --> 00:44:52,630
Microsoft kind of did the same thing, but they still call it Direct 3D.

442
00:44:52,630 --> 00:44:56,871
It's their the newest version of Direct 3D is lower level. It's called Direct 3D 12.

443
00:44:56,880 --> 00:45:01,430
And Mac looked at this and they decided we're going to do the same thing, too, but we're not going to use Vulcan.

444
00:45:01,430 --> 00:45:05,910
We're going to define our own and they call it metal.

445
00:45:05,910 --> 00:45:14,230
And so when we want to translate D3D12 into something that another operating system understands, that's probably Vulcan.

446
00:45:14,230 --> 00:45:15,990
And on Mac, we need to translate it to Metal somehow.

447
00:45:15,990 --> 00:45:23,829
And we decided instead of having a separate layer from D312 to Metal, we're just going to translate it to Vulcan and then translate the Vulcan to metal.

448
00:45:23,829 --> 00:45:31,190
And it also lets things written for Vulcan on Windows, which is also a thing that exists, that lets some work on Metal.

449
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:40,230
And having to do that translation, does that have a performance impact or is that not really thought?

450
00:45:40,230 --> 00:45:43,349
Yes, it's kind of like anything when you talk about performance.

451
00:45:43,349 --> 00:45:51,589
Like I mentioned this earlier, there's always going to be overhead from translating from one API to another,

452
00:45:51,589 --> 00:45:59,750
but we put in heroic efforts to try to make sure that doesn't matter to make sure that stuff that needs to be fast is really as fast as it can possibly be.

453
00:45:59,750 --> 00:46:02,230
And some very clever things have been done along those lines.

454
00:46:02,230 --> 00:46:10,630
And sometimes the you know the graphics drivers underneath are so good that it actually does run better even despite the translation overhead.

455
00:46:10,630 --> 00:46:20,630
And then sometimes to make it run fast we need to say well we're going to implement a new API that behaves more like Windows so we can do less work translating it.

456
00:46:20,630 --> 00:46:26,150
And sometimes that goes into the graphics library and sometimes that goes into other places.

457
00:46:26,160 --> 00:46:28,790
Yeah.

458
00:46:28,790 --> 00:46:35,109
Something I've found a little bit interesting about the last few years is developers in the past,

459
00:46:35,109 --> 00:46:43,829
they would generally target Windows and you might be lucky to get a Mac port or a Linux port.

460
00:46:43,829 --> 00:46:53,510
And I wonder like in your opinion now now that a lot of developers are just targeting Windows and relying on Wine or or Proton to run their software.

461
00:46:53,520 --> 00:47:02,230
Is there any I suppose downside to doing that or is it all just upside like everyone should target Windows as this common platform?

462
00:47:02,240 --> 00:47:03,990
Yeah, it's an interesting question.

463
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:10,630
There's some people who seem to think it's a bad thing that we're not getting native ports in the same sense.

464
00:47:10,630 --> 00:47:15,109
And then there's some people who see, no, that's a perfectly valid way to do ports.

465
00:47:15,109 --> 00:47:17,750
Just write for this de facto common API.

466
00:47:17,750 --> 00:47:23,829
It was never intended as a crossplatform common API, but we've made it one, right?

467
00:47:23,829 --> 00:47:29,270
And so why is that any worse than if it runs on a different API on Linux or Mac?

468
00:47:29,270 --> 00:47:31,109
And that argument tends to make sense to me.

469
00:47:31,109 --> 00:47:36,470
I don't personally see a lot of reason to say that one library is more pure than another.

470
00:47:36,470 --> 00:47:41,829
Right now, I do think Windows APIs are generally pretty bad.

471
00:47:41,829 --> 00:47:51,510
This might be, you know, just some sort of this might just be an effect of having to work with them for a very long time and see all their flaws and have to deal with the nonsense that they do.

472
00:47:51,510 --> 00:47:57,589
But I think that a lot of the native Linux APIs are better.

473
00:47:57,589 --> 00:48:05,670
But if you like your Windows API better and if you want to target Windows and that's the only way to do it, then sure, why not? What's wrong with that?

474
00:48:05,680 --> 00:48:08,309
Yeah.

475
00:48:08,309 --> 00:48:12,790
And I think doing it this way targeting Windows, I mean, if you look in the past,

476
00:48:12,790 --> 00:48:20,950
even though you had some software that would be ported to other operating systems without this compatibility layer,

477
00:48:20,950 --> 00:48:29,270
without people just targeting Windows, all this software that people can now run on these portable gaming handhelds or on Linux, most of that software was never going to be ported.

478
00:48:29,280 --> 00:48:31,110
Oh, yeah. Absolutely.

479
00:48:31,110 --> 00:48:38,790
That's kind of why Wine existed because people wanted to run their software that was never going to be ported.

480
00:48:38,790 --> 00:48:42,710
They just wanted and Ninja community just spent a lot of effort, you know, making all these individual programs run.

481
00:48:42,720 --> 00:48:44,470
Yeah.

482
00:48:44,470 --> 00:48:51,910
And I think it's pretty amazing too that now that's become this official way I suppose of distributing your software where Yeah.

483
00:48:51,920 --> 00:48:58,470
you say like, "Hey, I made a Windows version, but you're on your Linux machine.

484
00:48:58,470 --> 00:49:03,910
it's officially supported because we have this much belief in this compatibility layer.

485
00:49:03,920 --> 00:49:08,150
It's kind of incredible to see wine having got this far.

486
00:49:08,150 --> 00:49:15,270
I mean, I started working on it, you know, six, seven years ago and even then I could never have imagined it would be like this.

487
00:49:15,270 --> 00:49:23,829
So, as we wrap up, for the developers that are listening or or people who are just users of Wine,

488
00:49:23,829 --> 00:49:27,589
is there anything you think they should know about the project that we haven't talked about?

489
00:49:27,599 --> 00:49:29,910
I don't think there's anything I can think of.

490
00:49:29,920 --> 00:49:37,030
And if people want to learn more about the Wine Project or or see what you're up to, where should they head?

491
00:49:37,040 --> 00:49:41,670
We don't really have any things like news, unfortunately.

492
00:49:41,670 --> 00:49:43,670
Read the release notes.

493
00:49:43,670 --> 00:49:46,790
There are some people from codew weavers who do blogs.

494
00:49:46,790 --> 00:49:52,069
So if you go to codereavers.com/blog, there's some code wever stuff uh some marketing stuff,

495
00:49:52,069 --> 00:49:59,430
but there's also some developers who will talk about bugs that they are solving and how it's easy and and the experience of working on wine.

496
00:49:59,440 --> 00:50:10,069
And I suppose if someone's interested and like like let's say they have a piece of software, it's not working through wine,

497
00:50:10,069 --> 00:50:15,430
what's the best place for them to either get help or maybe even get involved with trying to fix it?

498
00:50:15,430 --> 00:50:23,750
So you can file a bug on winehq.org or find there's a lot of developer resources there

499
00:50:23,750 --> 00:50:33,670
and you can get involved with contributing to the software and there's links to our mailing list and IRC channels and the GitLab where all places you can find developers.

500
00:50:33,670 --> 00:50:35,750
We love to help you debug things.

501
00:50:35,750 --> 00:50:37,829
We love to help you fix things.

502
00:50:37,829 --> 00:50:47,671
We try our very best to be a welcoming community and we have a lot of experience working with people who want to get their application working. So we'd love to have another.

503
00:50:47,680 --> 00:50:50,790
Very cool.

504
00:50:50,790 --> 00:51:03,430
Yeah, I think wine is a really interesting project because I think for I guess it would have been for decades it seemed like very niche like not many people Oh yeah.

505
00:51:03,430 --> 00:51:14,790
were aware of it and now I think maybe in particular because of the Linux gaming handhelds like the Steam Deck Wine is now something that a bunch of people who would have never heard about it before now they're aware of it.

506
00:51:14,800 --> 00:51:17,030
Absolutely.

507
00:51:17,030 --> 00:51:20,470
I've watched that transformation happen in real time and it's been surreal.

508
00:51:20,480 --> 00:51:24,630
Very cool. Well, Elizabeth, thank you so much for joining me today.

509
00:51:24,640 --> 00:51:26,870
Thank you, Jeremy. I've been glad to be here.

